#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180506} :activity/id #uuid "b8ced6b0-8e1a-57eb-9f2f-b4bd8eace852" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050793 :topic/id #uuid "6768fffe-a644-5bd7-b35c-6b2cc2563786" :topic/math-academy-id 3716} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179606 :question/id #uuid "c1924df7-205f-512e-b024-72f5a654f062" :question/math-academy-id "e-9560"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262714}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "4fded59b-3b34-5c3a-b5bc-fbdf60f1028f" :step/math-academy-id 35826 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179607 :question/id #uuid "e9d8e6c2-d346-52fb-918a-5d5c95cd986c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9561"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048540}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262721}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form"} :step/id #uuid "a52a1b21-2c56-5112-aa12-6e43086599bc" :step/math-academy-id 35827 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180725 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ be a *closed* curve in the $xy$-plane. Recall that the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nCalculating flux using this definition is often cumbersome because we need to calculate $\\mathbf n,$ the outward-pointing unit normal vector. \n\nIn practice, we often use the following alternative formula to calculate the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\n**Watch Out!** When applying this formula, we must choose a positively-oriented parameterization for $C.$ This formula will *not* work if $C$ is negatively oriented!" :tutorial/id #uuid "c74e835b-f210-5a77-8d6e-aef474c89522" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5851 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cb889341-22d5-52e6-9979-1fd814bc5cec" :step/math-academy-id 35829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179608 :question/id #uuid "337d9d7e-5488-5e6d-af34-e01d22d3d0b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9574"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362552}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve"} :step/id #uuid "f9195b98-bf60-5d80-a9f1-4dbe4c7ec614" :step/math-academy-id 35863 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180510}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180727 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ through a piecewise-smooth, closed curve $C$ is a unique scalar number that represents the net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$ \n\nThe question now is, does the notion of flux exist for curves that are not closed?\n\nIndeed, it is possible to define the notion of flux through a non-closed curve. However, there is one crucial difference we need to bear in mind:\n\n- In the case of a smooth, closed curve, it is always clear which direction is \"out.\" Therefore, the outward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ is unique at every point on $C.$\n\n- However, on a smooth, non-closed curve, there is no clear notion of \"in\" and \"out.\" Instead, we can choose between *two* normal vectors at any point on the curve, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d1/d193eb8109ead15de50c06a7bb0b654243173d882bd361f9fd34026aa23c1b5f.png)\n\nTherefore, when computing the flux of a vector field through a non-closed curve, we first specify the direction that the unit normal points and then measure the flux relative to that direction. \n\nFor example, for the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and curve $C$ below, one choice for the unit normal will give a positive number, and the other a negative number.\n\n![](images/4f/4f92dd5334b5836703857c081049f126d39c141e84d8e94171e338a162cf3cc4.png)\n\nFinally, to calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across a non-closed curve $C,$ we can continue to use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** To apply this formula, we usually need to parameterize $C.$ It is *vital* that we pick a parameterization where the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ is a $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2794e26-620d-59e9-85de-a3bb49f6991a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5869 :tutorial/title "Calculating Flux for Non-Closed Curves"} :step/id #uuid "c6cc7967-4281-527a-8021-7768a792728f" :step/math-academy-id 35901 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180507}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180728 :tutorial/content "Let's now derive the following formula for the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that if $C$ is parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{y'(t)\\,\\mathbf i - x'(t)\\,\\mathbf j}{||\\mathbf r'(t)||},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $C$ is positively-oriented. \n\nSubstituting our expression for $\\mathbf n$ into our line integral definition of flux and using the fact that $\\textrm d s = ||\\mathbf r'(t)|| \\,\\textrm d t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{'}(t)\\,i-{x}^{'}(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{'}(t)||})||{r}^{'}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{'}(t)\\,i-{x}^{'}(t)\\,j}{\\cancel{||{r}^{'}(t)||}})\\cancel{||{r}^{'}(t)||}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot ({y}^{'}(t)\\,i-{x}^{'}(t)\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P(x(t),y(t)){y}^{'}(t)-Q(x(t),y(t)){x}^{'}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cc71a06d-4832-5949-91b4-71cb0f8185ca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5870 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Main Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7543ee2e-ca36-5209-8a6f-1fae02154a3d" :step/math-academy-id 35902}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179867}]}
